    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:46:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Vocational & Skills Training in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s vocational education pathways, community skills programmes, and the evidence needed to assess their employment outcomes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-pathways",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different pathways into practical learning",
        "content": "Technical and vocational education and training, usually shortened to TVET, encompasses several institutional routes in Iran. UNESCO-UNEVOC’s 2018 country profile distinguishes vocational secondary education, applied tertiary education, and non-formal training. In that account, schools fall under the Ministry of Education, tertiary provision under the Ministry of Science, Research and Technology, and non-formal provision under the Technical and Vocational Training Organization, or TVTO, within the labour ministry. These are dated institutional descriptions, rather than a verified register of arrangements in October 2026. [s1]\n\nThis distinction helps international readers interpret qualifications. A school diploma, a higher-education degree, and an occupational skills certificate belong to different educational contexts. They should not be treated as interchangeable simply because each involves practical subjects. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "skills-and-livelihoods",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Training can extend beyond a workshop",
        "content": "A UNFPA account published in November 2024 illustrates how vocational learning can be combined with other forms of support. Working with TVTO and the Ministry of Cooperatives, Labour and Social Welfare, the programme targeted female heads of household, unemployed young people, and people with disabilities in Kerman, Kohgiluyeh and Boyer-Ahmad, and West Azerbaijan. Its activities included locally relevant occupational learning and entrepreneurship support. [s2]\n\nThe same account describes a digital hub in Yasuj, the capital of Kohgiluyeh and Boyer-Ahmad province. Collaboration involving the State Welfare Organization and the online marketplace Digikala sought to help women, including women with disabilities, sell products online. Local instructors also received support in mentoring learners. This example shows that skills initiatives can address market access and teaching capacity alongside production techniques. [s2]\n\nUNFPA’s report is an implementing partner’s account. Its participant stories provide useful illustrations, but they do not establish average earnings gains or the durability of new businesses. The inspected article does not provide a comparison group or longitudinal employment evaluation. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "improving-delivery",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Improving the people who teach",
        "content": "Instructor development is another part of the vocational landscape. UNESCO Tehran and UNESCO-UNEVOC describe a project scheduled for 2023–2028 at Iran’s Instructor Training Center. Funded by the Korea International Cooperation Agency, it aims to improve facilities, teaching methods, and institutional capacity, while supporting young people through upskilling, guidance, and mentorship. These are project objectives; the project description does not demonstrate that every intended improvement has been completed. [s3]\n\nThe documented examples suggest a useful way to explore this subject: distinguish who provides training, what learners study, how competence is assessed, and what support connects learning to livelihoods. National conclusions about success require comparable evidence on completion, employment, and income. Those outcomes remain outside the evidence assembled for this overview.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
