    "description": "Skills training centres in Iran are learning settings for occupational instruction and related support, rather than a single contactable institution. Documented examples include public and private vocational providers, specialist instructor training, and locally organized recovery programmes. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Training centres", "Occupational skills", "Instructor training", "Local development", "Inclusive education"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:46:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:46:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Skills Training Centers in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover the different functions of Iranian skills training centres, from occupational learning to instructor preparation and community recovery."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "centres-and-functions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A centre is a learning setting, not a qualification",
        "content": "The term skills training centre describes a place or provider of learning, rather than one uniform credential. UNESCO-UNEVOC’s 2018 country profile records both public centres and private-sector vocational institutions within Iran’s non-formal training landscape. Its programme examples address jobseekers, existing workers, and rural populations. That historical account establishes diversity of provision, but it does not supply a current directory of courses or centres. [s1]\n\nCentres also serve different audiences. The Instructor Training Center in Karaj primarily prepares the people who teach vocational subjects. UNESCO-UNEVOC lists fields including welding, automotive technology, computing, pedagogy, and construction. Its role therefore differs from a local course aimed directly at an individual seeking an occupational skill. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "khoy-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Skills provision during community recovery",
        "content": "A UNESCO report published in January 2026 describes cooperation with TVTO after the earthquake in Khoy, in northwestern Iran. The initiative combined vocational and entrepreneurship learning with support for young people, women-headed households, and people with disabilities. Its account connects skills development with recovery from disruption, rather than treating instruction as an isolated classroom activity. [s3]\n\nThe reported activities included youth summer camps offering life skills, self-awareness, entrepreneurship, and psychosocial support. Entrepreneurs received workshops, mentorship, and start-up grants, while vocational staff attended training on disaster recovery and emergency planning. UNESCO also reports a July 2024 visit to the camp’s closing ceremony and visits to training centres in Urmia. These details locate the work in a specific regional recovery setting. [s3]\n\nThe account provides evidence of programme activities and support mechanisms. It does not establish how much participants’ incomes changed, how long businesses survived, or whether similar services were available at every centre in the region. Those questions require follow-up evidence beyond an activity report. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-certification",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Teaching, assessment, and subsequent work",
        "content": "Training participation and certification are separate steps. In its information about refugee livelihoods, UNHCR explains that trainees obtain official TVTO certificates after undertaking courses and successfully passing final examinations. Attendance alone should therefore not be described as proof of certified competence. [s4]\n\nA certificate also needs to be distinguished from access to employment. UNHCR’s guidance treats legal residence, work authorization, and occupational restrictions for foreign nationals separately from skills training. This page does not reproduce those changeable rules as individualized advice. The important editorial distinction is that learning a trade, demonstrating competence, and being authorized to undertake particular work are different matters. [s4]\n\nFor understanding a particular centre, the useful questions are concrete: whom does it teach, what practical learning does it provide, which body assesses learners, and what evidence exists about subsequent work? The inspected sources support examples of these functions, but do not establish a nationwide standard of facilities or outcomes.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
